# Default color database for dircolors (core-utils).
#
# Each line is "KEYWORD VALUE". VALUE is a list of terminal SGR codes
# separated by ';' (for example 01;34 is bold blue). A '#' ends the line's
# value, so everything after it is a comment.
#
# KEYWORD is a file-type name (DIR, LINK, ...), a file name pattern that
# starts with '*' (for example *~), or a file extension that starts with '.'
# (".gz" means the pattern "*.gz"). COLOR, OPTIONS and EIGHTBIT are accepted
# and ignored.
#
# COLORTERM and TERM lines take a glob pattern and test it against the
# environment variables of the same name. The first line that matches makes
# the entries after it apply; a later line that does not match switches them
# off again. The block below lists the terminals known to support color, so
# an unlisted terminal gets an empty LS_COLORS.
#
# The data follows the default database of GNU coreutils (master, checked
# 2026-11-04); the comments are our own.

# ---- terminals that get colors ----
COLORTERM ?*
TERM Eterm
TERM ansi
TERM *color*
TERM con[0-9]*x[0-9]*
TERM cons25
TERM console
TERM cygwin
TERM *direct*
TERM dtterm
TERM gnome
TERM hurd
TERM jfbterm
TERM konsole
TERM kterm
TERM linux
TERM linux-c
TERM mlterm
TERM putty
TERM rxvt*
TERM screen*
TERM st
TERM terminator
TERM tmux*
TERM vt100
TERM vt220
TERM xterm*

# ---- file types ----
RESET                 0         # reset to normal colors
DIR                   01;34     # directory
LINK                  01;36     # symbolic link
MULTIHARDLINK         00        # regular file with more than one hard link
FIFO                  40;33     # named pipe
SOCK                  01;35     # socket
DOOR                  01;35     # door (Solaris)
BLK                   40;33;01  # block device
CHR                   40;33;01  # character device
ORPHAN                40;31;01  # dangling symlink, or one that cannot be examined
MISSING               00        # the missing target of a dangling symlink
SETUID                37;41     # file with the set-user-ID bit
SETGID                30;43     # file with the set-group-ID bit
CAPABILITY            00        # file with capabilities (costly to look up)
STICKY_OTHER_WRITABLE 30;42     # directory with sticky bit, writable by others
OTHER_WRITABLE        34;42     # directory writable by others, without sticky bit
STICKY                37;44     # directory with sticky bit
EXEC                  01;32     # executable regular file

